WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:04.000
A splash of fresh water will reach the depths of your heart. Those mighty blue waves can propel you forward or they can calm and soothe. Coast to Michigan coast. Simply feel the flow in the moment. And let those vast lakes, rivers and streams create everlasting memories. Jump right in and let fresh wash over you in Pure Michigan. Keep it fresh at Michigan.org. Or on our website.
清凉的泉水将触及你心灵的深处。那些蔚蓝而雄伟的巨浪可以推动你向前，也能带来平静与慰藉。从密歇根的这片海岸到那片海岸。只需用心感受当下流淌的瞬间。让浩瀚的湖泊、河流和小溪勾勒出永恒的记忆。纵身跃入纯净的密歇根，让清新的水流洗涤你的心灵。访问 Michigan.org 或我们的网站，了解更多新鲜资讯。

2
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The Hume Freeway is one of the busiest interstate routes in Australia, linking the country's two most populous cities, Sydney and Melbourne. Stretching for 840 kilometres, the Hume is part of the Ozilink National Network, providing a vital connection for freight and transit between the two major East Coast cities. In 2013, the creation of the whole Brook Bypass marked the completion of an extensive overhaul of the roadway, with Sydney and Melbourne now linked by a continuous dual-lane freeway, unrestricted by the traffic lights and speed restrictions of passing townships. But it wasn't always this way.
休姆高速公路是澳大利亚最繁忙的城际公路之一，连接着该国人口最多的两个城市——悉尼和墨尔本。休姆高速公路全长 840 公里，是澳捷联国家网络的一部分，为两大东海岸城市之间的货运和交通提供了至关重要的连接。2013 年，整个布鲁克绕城公路的建成标志着这条公路大规模改造工程的完工，悉尼和墨尔本如今由一条连续的双车道高速公路相连，不再受过往城镇红绿灯和速度限制的约束。但情况并非一直如此。

3
00:01:46.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Prior to the upgrade, the Hume Freeway was the Hume Highway. Those navigating the old Hume had to drive through a vast number of urban and rural townships, traffic lights and intersections. Despite the bypass upgrades that occurred throughout the years, one aspect of the Hume has remained the same. It is the only road providing access to the Balingalos State Forest. Located 130 kilometres southwest of Sydney in the southern highlands region of New South Wales, Balingalos State Forest stretches for 40 square kilometres and comprises 700 hectares of commercial pine plantations, bordered by several thousand hectares of native bushland. Entrance is gained only by driving or walking through the seemingly endless rows of dense pine trees.
在升级之前，休姆高速公路曾是休姆公路。那些行驶在老休姆公路上的人必须穿过大量城市和乡村城镇、红绿灯以及十字路口。尽管多年来进行了绕城公路升级，但休姆公路有一个方面保持不变。它是通往巴林加洛斯国家森林的唯一道路。巴林加洛斯国家森林位于新南威尔士州南部高地地区，在悉尼西南 130 公里处，绵延 40 平方公里，包含 700 公顷的商业松树人工林，四周环绕着数千公顷的原生灌木林。进入该森林的唯一途径是驾车或步行穿过看似无尽的茂密松树林。

4
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:47.000
The forest is home to a plethora of native flora and fauna, including koalas, wombats, kangaroos and many species of birds. Providing a popular recreation spot for campers, trail bike riders, bushwalkers, picnickers and photographers, many parts of the seemingly endless rugged terrain are so remote that can only be accessed by a four-wheel drive or on foot. Entrance to Balingalos is gained via the Hume, 16 kilometres south of the southern highlands town of Bowerill, where a sealed road quickly gives way to rocky dirt roads, steep hills and creek crossings impossible to navigate without an off-road vehicle. Fire trails and a complex network of tracks and paths crisscross throughout the forest, which is so large and isolated that it's rare to encounter another person whilst visiting. There are no signs or obvious landmarks to signify one's location within, making it a very easy place to get disoriented and lost.
这片森林是众多本土动植物的家园，包括考拉、袋熊、袋鼠和许多鸟类。这里是露营者、越野自行车手、丛林徒步者、野餐者和摄影师的热门休闲胜地，看似无尽的崎岖地形中有许多地方非常偏远，只能驾驶四驱车或步行才能到达。进入巴林加洛斯需要经由休姆公路，在南部高地城镇鲍威利以南 16 公里处，铺砌的公路很快就会变成岩石泥路、陡峭的山丘和不驾驶越野车根本无法通过的溪流。森林中纵横交错着防火道以及复杂的轨道和路径，这里如此之大且与世隔绝，以至于在游览时很难遇到其他人。里面没有任何标志或明显的路标来标明自己所在的位置，这使得这里成为一个非常容易迷失方向和迷路的地方。

5
00:03:47.000 --> 00:05:27.000
The forest is thought to have been named by an early 1800s settler who was so enchanted by the untamed beauty of the area that he christened at Balingalo, meaning Good Angel. Despite its splendour and distinctively Australian landscape which continues to attract nature lovers and outdoor enthusiasts from all over the world, those who venture to the secluded forest note the vast space is enveloped by neary silence. As visitors turn to the forest entrance, a large ominous sign surrounded by a wall of towering trees warns. Welcome to Balingalo State Forest. Please be careful. In the 1990s, over 200,000 young travellers ventured to Australia each year. Attracted by the sun, surf and rugged natural beauty of the country's varied landscape, travellers were promised exotic and exciting adventures. The backpacking lifestyle offered young people the opportunity to develop their independence, gain new experiences and interact with a wide range of new people from all over.
人们认为这片森林是以 19 世纪初的一位定居者的名字命名的，他被该地区未受破坏的美景深深迷住，于是将其命名为巴林加洛，意为“好天使”。尽管它拥有壮丽的景色和独特的澳大利亚景观，不断吸引着来自世界各地的自然爱好者和户外运动爱好者，但那些冒险进入这片人迹罕至的森林的人注意到，这片广阔的空间被近乎绝对的寂静所笼罩。当游客转向森林入口时，一堵高耸的树墙包围着一个巨大而不祥的标志，上面警告着：“欢迎来到巴林加洛斯国家森林。请小心。”20 世纪 90 年代，每年有超过 20 万年轻游客前往澳大利亚。由于被该国多变景观的阳光、冲浪和崎岖的自然美景所吸引，游客们被承诺会有异国情调和令人兴奋的冒险。背包客的生活方式为年轻人提供了培养独立性、获得新体验以及与来自各地的形形色色的人互动的机会。

6
00:05:27.000 --> 00:07:35.000
Most backpackers who arrived to Australia were aged in their early to mid-20s and either taking time off between studying or securing their first professional jobs or wanting to figure out their next step in life. A majority travelled alone or with one other person, stayed in the country for an average visit of six months and either lived frugally off their hard-earned savings or funded their trip as they went with fruit-picking jobs, bar shifts, nannying or casual farm work. 40% of backpackers came from the UK and another 30% ventured over from continental Europe. Backpacking accounted for a major part of the country's tourism industry, contributing approximately $1.5 billion to the Australian economy annually. In the days before the internet, backpackers relied on word of mouth to plan their trips. Knowledge was passed from one traveller to the next, the best destinations to visit, the areas to avoid and the cheapest ways to get from place to place. Noticeboards on display at hostels provided an invaluable resource to tourists seeking travel companions or looking to share the cost of transit with many backpackers partnering up to cut costs and make new friends. Trains and buses were a common form of transport but many budget travellers also purchased cheaper used cars so they could cover more ground at their own pace. Although it was often frowned upon, hitchhiking provided another popular method of transit. Hitching provided a cheap, convenient way to move around for those on tight budgets while allowing visitors to meet locals and learn all about the area they were traversing by the people who lived there. Although this risky practice was strongly discouraged by police and many employers advised truck drivers against picking up hitchhikers, in the 90s it was a common way for travellers to get around.
大多数抵达澳大利亚的背包客年龄在 20 岁出头到 25 岁左右，他们要么是在学业结束后或找到第一份正式工作前休假，要么是想弄清楚人生的下一步。大多数人独自旅行或与另外一人同行，平均在澳大利亚停留六个月，要么靠辛苦赚来的积蓄过着简朴的生活，要么通过摘水果、调酒、保姆或临时农场工作来边走边赚维持旅费。40% 的背包客来自英国，另有 30% 来自欧洲大陆。背包客是该国旅游业的重要组成部分，每年为澳大利亚经济贡献约 15 亿美元。在没有互联网的年代，背包客依靠口耳相传来规划行程。知识在旅行者之间传递：最佳旅游目的地、应避开的区域以及从一个地方到另一个地方的最便宜方式。青年旅舍里张贴的公告栏为寻找旅伴或希望分摊交通费用的游客提供了宝贵的资源，许多背包客结伴同行以削减成本并结交新朋友。火车和公共汽车是常见的交通方式，但许多经济型旅行者也会购买更便宜的二手车，以便按自己的节奏游览更多地方。虽然这种做法经常受到非议，但搭便车提供了另一种受欢迎的交通方式。对于预算紧张的人来说，搭便车提供了一种便宜、便捷的出行方式，同时也让游客能够结识当地人，并通过居住在那里的人了解他们正在穿越南北的地区。尽管警察强烈反对这种危险的做法，许多雇主也建议卡车司机不要搭载搭便车的人，但在 90 年代，这是旅行者出行的常见方式。

7
00:07:35.000 --> 00:09:22.000
A free book titled The Budget Travellers Guidebook was available in hostels throughout New South Wales providing information written by backpackers for backpackers. It listed the Hume Highway as an ideal and popular hitchhiking spot for those looking to travel south from Sydney to Melbourne, noting the Liverpool train station as a good starting point. The guidebook advised, hitchhiking is generally safe and legal if you stand in a sensible spot. Solo guys and couples have a good chance of a lift. Make a destination sign and have your backpack visible. Women should not hitchhike alone. 19-year-old James Gibson was one of many young travellers who favoured hitchhiking as a mode of transit. Described as friendly, free-spirited, impulsive and somewhat nomadic, James was most passionate about two things in life, art and the environment. He took conservation issues seriously and was dedicated to saving the planet by attending wilderness walks, volunteering in the country fire service and participating in anti-logging protests. His dedication to the cause had recently led him to hitchhike from his family home on Victoria's Mornington Peninsula to a protest in the New South Wales town of Nimbun. Upon his return home, James was happy to be reunited with his family, of whom he was very close to. Yet, the nomadic lifestyle called to him and he soon found himself eager to hit the road again.
新南威尔士州的青年旅舍里提供一本名为《预算旅行者指南》的免费书籍，其中包含了由背包客为背包客撰写的信息。书中将休姆公路列为那些希望从悉尼向南前往墨尔本的人理想且热门的搭便车地点，并指出利物浦火车站是一个不错的起点。该指南建议：“如果您站在合理的位置，搭便车通常是安全且合法的。独自旅行的男性和情侣搭到顺风车的几率很高。制作一个目的地标志并让您的背包显眼。女性不应独自搭便车。”19 岁的詹姆斯·吉布森是喜欢以搭便车作为交通方式的众多年轻旅行者之一。詹姆斯被描述为友好、崇尚自由、冲动且有些四海为家，他生命中最热衷的两件事是艺术和环境。他认真对待自然保护问题，通过参加荒野徒步、在乡村消防局做志愿者以及参与反砍伐森林抗议活动来致力于拯救地球。他对这项事业的奉献最近促使他从位于维多利亚州莫宁顿半岛的家人住所搭便车前往新南威尔士州宁宾镇参加抗议活动。回到家后，詹姆斯很高兴能与他非常亲密的家人团聚。然而，这种游牧般的生活方式召唤着他，他很快发现自己渴望再次踏上旅程。

8
00:09:22.000 --> 00:11:20.000
He decided to defer the sculpture course he had enrolled in at Frankston Tafe to have one more adventure. Although he was happy travelling alone, James invited his girlfriend Deborah Everest to join him on the trip. The two had met earlier that year at the beginning of 1989, where they had hit it off immediately despite their many differences. Deborah Everest was a bubbly and vivacious 19-year-old noted for her dry, mischievous sense of humour. A budding writer and avid reader, she was studying psychology at Melbourne's Monash University while working part-time for charity organisation The Quadriplegic Society. Deborah intended to eventually make the move into journalism. She had been living away from home while attending uni, but as 1989 drew to an end, she deferred her studies and returned to her family home in suburban Frankston to help take care of her father after he was diagnosed with cancer. Deborah planned to return to university the following year, but with her studies temporarily on hold, she was free to join James on his journey. Although she didn't share the same nomadic hippie traits as her boyfriend and much preferred home comforts, Deborah had never travelled before and thought it would do her good to take a break and broaden her horizons. James and Deborah made a plan to travel north to Sydney, where they would catch up with some of James' friends before the group would head back south together to Albury, a regional city on the New South Wales-Victorian border. There, they'd make their way to the small riverside town of Walwa to attend Confest, a bush camping festival that celebrates alternative lifestyles, offering workshops, live music and vegetarian food.
他决定推迟在弗兰克斯顿技术与继续教育学院报名的雕塑课程，再去进行一次冒险。尽管他很享受独自旅行，詹姆斯还是邀请了他的女友黛博拉·埃弗雷斯加入他的旅程。两人于当年早些时候（1989 年初）相遇，尽管他们有许多差异，但他们一拍即合。黛博拉·埃弗雷斯是一位性格开朗、活泼的 19 岁女孩，以其冷幽默和恶作剧式的幽默感而闻名。作为一名崭露头角的作家和狂热的读者，她在墨尔本的莫纳什大学攻读心理学，同时为慈善机构“四肢瘫痪者协会”兼职工作。黛博拉原本打算最终转行从事新闻工作。上大学期间她一直离家居住，但随着 1989 年接近尾声，她推迟了学业，返回弗兰克斯顿郊区的家中，以便在父亲被诊断出患有癌症后照顾他。黛博拉计划第二年重返大学，但在学业暂时搁置期间，她可以自由地加入詹姆斯的旅程。尽管她没有像男友那样四海为家的嬉皮士特质，而且更喜欢家庭的舒适，但黛博拉此前从未旅行过，她认为休息一下并开阔眼界对自己有好处。詹姆斯和黛博拉计划向北前往悉尼，在那里他们会与詹姆斯的一些朋友会合，然后一行人将一起向南返回阿尔伯里——新南威尔士州和维多利亚州边境的一个区域城市。在那里，他们将前往河畔小镇瓦尔瓦参加“音乐节”，这是一个庆祝另类生活方式的丛林露营节，提供工作坊、现场音乐和素食。

9
00:11:20.000 --> 00:12:51.000
On the morning of December 28, 1989, James and Deborah's parents respectively dropped their children off at the Frankston train station. James told his mother the pair intended to hitchhike from Melbourne to Sydney via the Hume Highway, and she wasn't happy about the idea. Although her son was a gregarious and experienced traveller, she was aware of the dangers of hitchhiking and warned her son to be careful. James reassured his mother that hitchhiking was perfectly safe, as long as he didn't travel alone, so she had nothing to worry about, considering Deborah would be by his side. Unlike James, Deborah didn't tell her mother they planned on hitchhiking, instead explaining they were catching a train to Melbourne and would then be getting a lift to Sydney with some friends. At around 9am, James and Deborah farewelled their parents, purchased their tickets and boarded the train to Melbourne. James had packed his red with blue trim Berghaus brand backpack full of clothes, as well as a small camping stove and a 35mm Ricoh brand SLR camera his parents had given him earlier that year. Deborah was equipped with a green nylon sports bag packed with clothes and personal items, a green and beige sleeping bag that she had taken from her brother without asking, and $60 in cash.
1989 年 12 月 28 日清晨，詹姆斯和黛博拉的父母分别将各自的孩子送到弗兰克斯顿火车站。詹姆斯告诉母亲，两人打算经由休姆公路从墨尔本搭便车去悉尼，而她对这个想法并不高兴。尽管她的儿子是一个热情且经验丰富的旅行者，但她意识到了搭便车的危险，并警告儿子要小心。詹姆斯向母亲保证搭便车绝对安全，只要他不是独自旅行，所以她没有什么好担心的，因为黛博拉会陪在他身边。与詹姆斯不同，黛博拉并没有告诉母亲他们计划搭便车，而是解释说他们正乘火车去墨尔本，然后会和一些朋友一起搭车去悉尼。上午 9 点左右，詹姆斯和黛博拉与父母告别，买好票并登上了去墨尔本的火车。詹姆斯收拾了一个带有蓝色饰边的红色巴古斯牌背包，里面装满了衣服，还有一个小型露营炉以及父母在当年早些时候送给他的一台 35 毫米理光牌单反相机。黛博拉则携带了一个装有衣服和个人物品的绿色尼龙运动包，一个她未经允许从哥哥那里拿来的绿米相间睡袋，以及 60 美元现金。

10
00:12:51.000 --> 00:14:00.000
The duo disembarked the train in Melbourne City and quickly succeeded in obtaining a ride northwards to Sydney. It was the summer holidays, so there was plenty of traffic on the road, and it was easy to get a lift. James and Deborah made the long journey north without any hiccups and arrived in Sydney as planned. Upon their arrival at James's friend's house in the inner city suburb of Surrey Hills, they discovered the group they were supposed to be meeting there had already left for Confest without them. Their trip to Sydney was now pointless as James and Deborah had specifically travelled an additional six hours north to join up with the Confest group. A little frustrated but accepting the situation, James and Deborah decided to stay the night in Sydney before making the six hour journey back south to Confest in the morning. James' friend's flatmate invited them to sleep on the lounge room floor before they departed for Orbury the next day. That night, December 29, Deborah phoned her mother to say,
两人在墨尔本市下车，并迅速成功搭上了向北前往悉尼的顺风车。当时正值暑假，路上车流量很大，搭车很容易。詹姆斯和黛博拉顺利完成了向北的长途旅行，按计划抵达了悉尼。当他们抵达位于内城萨里山郊区的詹姆士朋友家时，他们发现本应在那里会合的小组已经先于他们前往了音乐节。他们去悉尼的行程现在变得毫无意义，因为詹姆斯和黛博拉特意向北多行驶了六个小时，就是为了跟音乐节小组会合。虽然有些沮丧，但接受了现实，詹姆斯和黛博拉决定在悉尼过夜，第二天一早再向南行驶六个小时赶往音乐节。詹姆斯朋友的室友邀请他们在第二天出发去奥尔伯里之前睡在客厅的地板上。那天晚上，即 12 月 29 日，黛博拉给母亲打电话说：

11
00:14:00.000 --> 00:15:09.000
We've arrived safely. I knew you would be in a panic until you heard from me. We're fine. Don't worry. I'll send you a postcard and we'll ring you tomorrow. James and Deborah set out for Orbury the following morning of December 30 at around 10am. They intended to follow the well-worn Backpackers path which involved catching a train to either Liverpool or Kasula in Sydney's south-west before heading towards the Hume Highway on foot and then attempting to hitch a ride south from a passing motorist. However, the exact route they ultimately decided to take is unknown. That day, Deborah's mother, Patricia Everest, waited eagerly for a phone call from her daughter as promised. But the evening came and went and there was no word from Deborah. Even though it was unlike Deborah not to be in touch, Patricia tried to reassure herself that the travelling couple were probably just having such a great time that they forgot to call home.
“我们安全到达了。我知道在你听到我的消息之前一定会惊慌失措。我们很好。别担心。我会给你寄一张明信片，我们明天给你打电话。”第二天（即 12 月 30 日）上午 10 点左右，詹姆斯和黛博拉动身前往奥尔伯里。他们打算沿着那条备受背包客喜爱的路线：乘火车前往悉尼西南部的利物浦或卡苏拉，然后步行前往休姆公路，接着尝试向南搭乘过往司机的顺风车。然而，他们最终选择的具体路线不得而知。那天，黛博拉的母亲帕特里夏·埃弗雷斯焦急地等待着女儿如约打来的电话。但傍晚来来去去，却没有黛博拉的任何消息。尽管黛博拉不联系家人很不符合她的习惯，但帕特里夏还是试图安慰自己，认为这对旅行的情侣可能只是玩得太开心以至于忘记给家里打电话了。

12
00:15:09.000 --> 00:16:07.000
As the days went by and still she hadn't heard from her daughter, Patricia became increasingly concerned. She contacted James' mother, Peg Gibson, who advised she hadn't received any contact from her son. Peg wasn't too worried though. She was used to James venturing off to remote locations where there were limited means of communication. So she assured Patricia their children were probably just in an area with no telephone access. But as the days continued to pass by and still there was no word from James or Deborah, Peg began to share Patricia's concern. The two mothers kept in close contact, see if the other had heard anything. But when no news came through, they soon became frantic. On January 15, 1990, two weeks since Deborah's last phone call home,
